; Falx — pursuit interceptor (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P4.2, the fifth roster type).
;
; The design in one line: "it out-runs everything and out-turns nothing."
; Every number below serves that sentence, and the two that matter most are
; `speed` and `accel` READ TOGETHER:
;
;   speed 25 = 1.8x the raider's 14, exactly as P4.2 specifies.
;   accel 11 = LOWER than the raider's 18, and that is the whole design.
;
; `accel` is what `velocity.move_toward` spends to CHANGE direction, so a fast
; body with low accel physically cannot turn tightly — the wide arc falls out of
; the physics instead of being scripted. That is what makes the counterplay real:
; bait the pass, then kill it during the long recovery it cannot shorten.
;
; `preferred_range` is re-read for this type: not an orbit radius (it never
; orbits) but the BREAK-OFF distance where the pass ends and the climb begins.
;
; fire_rate 4.0 looks alarming beside the raider's 1.5 and is not: the falx only
; shoots inside a narrow cone during the run-in, so its damage arrives in short
; committed bursts with long quiet gaps. Average output over a fight is far below
; the raider's — the burst is the threat, the uptime is the weakness.
;
; hull 26 is under the raider's 40 (P4.2: "light — fragile is the price of
; speed"), and it is what makes the flak `++` on the P4.3 row achievable: one
; burst that catches the pass should end it.
